# 2. First action: assess the existing application before changing it

Before implementing anything, inspect the complete repository and document the actual current design.

## 2.1 Repository assessment

Identify:
- Frontend framework, backend framework, application entry points and runtime/deployment model.
- Current price source(s), libraries, endpoints, API keys, symbol mappings and polling/subscription mechanisms.
- All locations that retrieve, transform, cache, store, calculate, display or publish gold prices.
- Existing market-data adapters, domain models, caches, background workers, event bus, WebSocket/SSE channels and scheduled tasks.
- Current use of Yahoo Finance, `yfinance`, broker APIs, public endpoints, static fixtures, demo data, mock services or hard-coded fallback values.
- Current symbol used for “gold”, such as XAU/USD spot, a broker-specific XAUUSD contract, a COMEX futures contract, a gold ETF, or a CFD quote.
- Whether a displayed value is bid, ask, midpoint, last trade, settlement, close, indicative reference price or a calculated conversion.
- Timezone assumptions, timestamp parsing, polling intervals, cache TTLs, retry logic, stale-data handling and UI refresh behaviour.
- Duplicate price-fetching logic across dashboard cards, charts, technical indicators, strategies, reports, AI agents, notifications and APIs.
- Existing secrets/configuration handling and current test coverage.

Search the codebase for all references to gold symbols, quote fields, price endpoints, provider names, “live”, “real-time”, timestamps, caches and fallback values. Trace one price from its upstream source all the way to every user-facing consumer.

## 2.2 Produce a concise defect report

Before or alongside implementation, record:
- The exact observed incorrect value and the intended instrument.
- The provider and endpoint currently used.
- The upstream response received, with credentials and sensitive headers removed.
- The normalized value and every transformation applied.
- The displayed value and its source timestamp.
- The most likely root cause and the evidence supporting it.
- Whether the cause is a wrong symbol, stale cache, delayed feed, wrong field, incorrect unit/currency, timezone issue, parsing bug, inverted conversion, bad fallback, duplicate state, or another issue.
- The corrective change and regression tests.

Do not invent a root cause. Prove it with code inspection, captured sanitized responses and repeatable tests.

## 2.3 Preserve existing working features

Do not rewrite the application from scratch. Reuse and improve the existing module boundaries. If the repository already has an appropriate market-data service, extend it rather than creating a second service with overlapping responsibilities.

Before adding a dependency, provider adapter, database table, background worker or configuration screen, check whether the capability already exists.

