# 6. Data-quality controls and price correctness

Build one reusable validation pipeline that runs before data reaches any consumer.

## 6.1 Validation checks

Validate:
- Canonical instrument and provider symbol.
- Expected currency and unit.
- Positive and finite prices.
- Bid/ask consistency when both exist.
- Negative, zero, null, NaN, infinity and malformed numeric values.
- Timestamp validity, ordering and age.
- Duplicate messages and out-of-order updates.
- Unusually large price jumps or spreads, using configurable robust thresholds.
- Stale values repeatedly returned as if fresh.
- Currency conversion direction and conversion timestamp where conversion is used.
- Price precision and rounding rules.
- Futures contract expiry/roll continuity when futures are supported.
- Provider response schema changes.
- Source-to-source discrepancies for genuinely comparable quotes.

A suspicious jump must not automatically be discarded if it may be a real market event. Mark it as anomalous, compare independent evidence if configured, and apply a transparent policy. All thresholds must be instrument-aware and configurable.

## 6.2 No silent corrections

Do not silently “fix” a provider quote by applying an unexplained multiplier, offset, conversion or symbol substitution. Every transformation must be explicit, documented, testable and auditable.

If USD per troy ounce is converted to another unit/currency, use a named conversion service and a timestamped FX rate. Show the original quote and converted quote as separate values with the formula and conversion provenance available in diagnostics.

## 6.3 Downstream consistency

All of the following must consume the same validated canonical data service:
- Live price card and overview dashboard.
- Price chart and candles.
- Technical indicators and multi-timeframe analysis.
- Strategies, entry/exit levels and risk calculations.
- AI agents and market commentary.
- Backtests, clearly separated from live data.
- Reports, downloadable exports and historical event analysis.
- Telegram/Discord/Slack/email/webhook notifications.
- API endpoints and status screens.

No page, agent or alert may call a provider directly or independently calculate a conflicting “current gold price” outside the canonical service. Provider-specific code belongs inside its adapter.

