# 10. Telegram and multi-channel notifications

Build a reusable notification subsystem. It must support both market/advisory messages and operational/app-status messages without duplicating formatting, routing, retry logic or delivery records.

## 10.1 Architecture

Create or extend the following responsibilities within the existing architecture:
- `NotificationService` — application-facing entry point.
- `NotificationEvent` — canonical event schema.
- `NotificationTemplateRegistry` — shared message templates and versioning.
- `NotificationPolicy` — severity, user preferences, quiet hours, deduplication and rate limits.
- `NotificationRouter` — maps event types and severity to configured destinations.
- `NotificationChannel` interface — provider-neutral delivery contract.
- Channel adapters — Telegram, Discord, Slack, email, generic HTTPS webhook and any additional channel only when its API, access and terms have been verified.
- `NotificationDeliveryRepository` — delivery state and retry metadata.
- `NotificationHealthService` — channel connectivity and delivery monitoring.

These names are guidance, not a mandate to over-engineer. Use the existing module system and implement each capability once.

## 10.2 Telegram adapter

Implement Telegram using the official Bot API:
- Configurable bot token and destination chat/channel ID.
- Explicit setup instructions for creating a bot and obtaining the destination chat ID.
- Connection/test-message action.
- Separate notification categories and destination routing if the configuration supports multiple chats.
- Message templates for application status, provider failure/recovery, stale data, quote anomalies and advisory signals.
- Safe handling of Telegram rate limits, timeouts, network failures and API errors.
- Message length handling, escaping/formatting rules and safe links.
- Delivery result recording and retry with bounded exponential backoff and jitter.
- Deduplication and rate limiting to prevent notification floods.
- Optional quiet hours and user timezone.
- Clear “configured”, “tested”, “enabled” and “delivery healthy” states.

Never include the bot token in message content, UI output, logs or exception text. Treat the bot token like a password. A test must send a real test message only after the user explicitly clicks the test action or enables the channel.

## 10.3 Additional notification channels

Use the same channel contract for:
- Discord incoming webhooks.
- Slack incoming webhooks or supported official app/bot integrations.
- Email via a configurable SMTP service or supported email provider.
- Generic HTTPS webhooks with configurable URL, authentication and payload schema.
- Other channels (for example WhatsApp or mobile push) only as optional future adapters after reviewing current official API requirements, costs, account approval, template rules and regional availability.

Do not claim that a channel is ready merely because its configuration form exists. Each enabled adapter must have a real integration test and clear operational status.

## 10.4 Notification event catalogue

Support configurable notification rules for at least the following:

### Application lifecycle
- Application started successfully.
- Application stopped gracefully, where a shutdown event can be reliably sent.
- Application version/deployment changed.
- Background worker started, stopped or failed.
- Scheduled job failed or recovered.
- Database/cache or required dependency became unavailable or recovered.

### Market-data operations
- Primary provider disconnected or authentication failed.
- Instrument mapping unsupported or invalid.
- Live-data entitlement/subscription missing.
- Valid quote received after startup.
- Quote became stale.
- Quote became unavailable.
- Fallback provider activated.
- Primary provider recovered and passed validation.
- Data source changed.
- Source discrepancy exceeds threshold.
- Timestamp, unit, currency, spread or price anomaly detected.
- Market/session opened or closed, when reliable calendar data exists.
- Repeated rate-limit response or provider quota warning.
- Data quality restored after an incident.

### Advisory events
- New validated bullish/bearish/neutral advisory.
- Advisory updated, invalidated or expired.
- Entry trigger reached, if this alert is explicitly enabled.
- Stop/target level touched based on validated data, if enabled.
- `WAIT`, `NO TRADE` or `INSUFFICIENT DATA` status when useful and enabled.
- Major scheduled macroeconomic event approaching, if calendar source and timezone are validated.
- A high-priority risk warning or recommendation suppression.

Do not emit an advisory signal just because the price changed. Follow the canonical recommendation engine, data-quality gates and notification policy.

## 10.5 Notification content standards

A price/advisory notification should include, when relevant:
- Instrument name/type.
- Observed price and price type.
- Currency/unit.
- Provider and data classification.
- Source timestamp and quote age.
- Direction/status and timeframe.
- Entry/stop/targets only if generated by the approved advisory engine.
- Risk/reward assumptions and invalidation conditions.
- Short explanation and link to the relevant app screen/report.
- Unique event ID and generated-at timestamp.
- Clear statement if data is delayed, stale, indicative or otherwise limited.

Never send a stale or unverified price in a message that labels it as current. If a source becomes stale, send a data-quality/availability alert rather than a fabricated market update.

## 10.6 Severity, routing and noise control

Provide event severity levels such as:
- `INFO`
- `SUCCESS`
- `WARNING`
- `ERROR`
- `CRITICAL`

Allow users to configure by event category:
- Enable/disable.
- Destination channel(s).
- Severity threshold.
- Quiet hours and timezone.
- Cooldown/deduplication window.
- Maximum messages per time interval.
- Escalation after repeated failures.
- Recovery notification behaviour.
- Whether routine advisories should be grouped into a digest.

Operational incidents such as invalid data or loss of feed may need immediate delivery even during quiet hours, depending on user policy. Do not hard-code this exception without an explicit setting.

Avoid repeated messages for the same incident. Prefer “incident opened”, periodic summary if configured, and “incident recovered” over sending an alert on every polling cycle.

## 10.7 Delivery reliability

- Persist notification events before delivery where practical.
- Use a durable queue/outbox pattern if the existing stack supports it and reliability requirements justify it.
- Track `pending`, `sent`, `failed`, `retry_scheduled`, `suppressed` and `dead_letter` states.
- Use bounded retries and exponential backoff with jitter.
- Respect provider-specific rate limits.
- Make retries idempotent where possible.
- Record destination, attempt count, timestamps, provider response category and sanitized error.
- Do not retry permanent errors forever.
- Provide a dead-letter/review view for unresolved deliveries.
- Ensure a notification-channel failure does not crash market-data processing or the UI.
- Avoid recursive alert loops in which notification failure generates unlimited notifications about notification failure.

