# 16. Performance and resilience requirements

- Use asynchronous I/O where appropriate to the existing framework.
- Set explicit connection and response timeouts.
- Avoid blocking UI requests while waiting for a provider.
- Use a shared ingestion task or streaming subscription rather than having every browser tab independently poll the upstream provider.
- Coalesce equivalent in-flight requests where appropriate.
- Apply caching with explicit TTL and original timestamps.
- Respect provider limits and terms.
- Avoid unbounded worker queues and memory growth.
- Reconnect streaming clients with bounded backoff and jitter.
- Ensure subscriptions are not duplicated after reconnect or page refresh.
- Prevent a slow provider from blocking a healthy provider.
- Apply circuit breakers where justified and expose their state.
- Make manual refresh actions safe to repeat.
- Document expected resource usage and operational limits.

Polling interval, source update frequency and source latency are separate values. A browser refreshing every second does not make a source that updates every minute real-time.

