# 17. Implementation sequence

Work in controlled, testable phases. Do not skip the defect investigation.

## Phase 1 — Reproduce and diagnose
- Inspect repository and current source.
- Reproduce the incorrect rate.
- Trace symbol, payload, transformations, cache and UI.
- Identify root cause and document it.
- Add a failing regression test where feasible.

## Phase 2 — Canonical instrument and quote contract
- Confirm the intended default gold instrument.
- Define canonical instrument identity and quote schema.
- Correct symbol mappings, units, currency, timestamps and price type.
- Remove or isolate any unsafe hard-coded/mock fallback.

## Phase 3 — Canonical market-data service
- Implement or repair provider adapters and routing.
- Add normalization, quality checks, freshness rules and provenance.
- Implement cache semantics, bounded retries and eligible fallback.
- Ensure every consumer uses this service.

## Phase 4 — User configuration and status UI
- Add Market Data & Connections.
- Add provider selection, instrument mapping, connection/quote tests and diagnostics.
- Add explicit live/delayed/stale/cached/unavailable labels.
- Add actionable error states.

## Phase 5 — Optional broker adapters
- Verify current official API capabilities and account requirements.
- Implement only adapters that support the selected product.
- Verify market-data permissions and live/delayed status.
- Keep all brokerage operations read-only under this addendum.

## Phase 6 — Notification engine
- Implement shared event, policy, routing, template, delivery and retry components.
- Implement Telegram.
- Add other channels only where required and supported.
- Add configuration UI, test actions, status and delivery history.
- Wire application, market-data and advisory events into the shared notification engine.

## Phase 7 — Observability and security
- Add health endpoints, metrics, structured logs and sanitized diagnostics.
- Review secret storage, access controls, SSRF protection and audit history.
- Document alerting limitations and provider licensing assumptions.

## Phase 8 — Verification and release
- Run all existing tests and new tests.
- Run lint, type checks, static analysis and dependency/security checks supported by the project.
- Run end-to-end and manual acceptance tests.
- Review UI responsiveness and accessibility.
- Provide the defect report, test results, configuration guide, release notes and known limitations.

If time or scope requires staged delivery, prioritize correct price identity, source provenance, freshness, safe failure and consistent downstream use before adding extra channels or nonessential UI polish.

