# 20. Developer execution instructions

Act as the senior engineering team responsible for fixing an existing production-facing application.

1. Start by inspecting the repository, tests, runtime configuration and current market-data flow.
2. Reproduce the wrong price and prove its root cause.
3. Read the current official provider/API documentation and terms for each integration you intend to implement. Do not rely on outdated snippets or unverified third-party claims.
4. Make the smallest sound architectural changes that fully solve the problem.
5. Reuse the existing market-data, domain, persistence, UI, event and configuration components wherever appropriate.
6. Keep provider-specific logic inside adapters and use one canonical quote schema and validation path.
7. Keep notification formatting, routing, delivery and retries in one shared notification subsystem.
8. Do not invent provider capabilities, market prices, entitlements or successful delivery.
9. Do not silently substitute an instrument or claim that delayed data is live.
10. Do not use hard-coded values or mocks in production mode. Any demo mode must be unmistakably labelled and technically isolated.
11. Do not expose secrets or enable live order execution.
12. Add tests before claiming the defect is fixed.
13. Run the actual project test, lint, type-check and build commands; report real results, including failures.
14. Update documentation and configuration examples alongside the implementation.
15. End with a concise implementation summary: root cause, files/modules changed, source selected and why, observed quote classification, provider/broker setup, notification setup, test results, known limitations and recommended next step.

**Final engineering principle:** one canonical instrument identity, one validated market-data pipeline, one honest freshness model, one reusable notification engine, and transparent operational status across the entire application.
